    <section class="glossary-section" id="a">
      <h2>A</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>A/B test</dt><dd>Showing two versions of a page (or of a button, headline, or image) to different visitors to see which works better. It's useful once a site has steady traffic, and rarely useful for a brand-new local site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Above the fold</dt><dd>The part of a page you see without scrolling. The term comes from newspapers, and it still matters: most visitors decide whether to stay based on the first screen.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Accessibility</dt><dd>Building a site so everyone can use it, including people who use screen readers, a keyboard instead of a mouse, or magnification. I build every site to WCAG 2.2 AA as a baseline.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Alt text</dt><dd>A short written description attached to an image. Screen readers read it aloud, and it appears if the image fails to load. Meaningful images get descriptive alt text, and purely decorative images are hidden from assistive technology.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Anchor link</dt><dd>A link that jumps to a specific spot on a page (for example, <code>/faq/#pricing</code>). It's useful on long pages and in the tables of contents on my technical guides.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Anchor text</dt><dd>The visible, clickable words of a link. Good anchor text says where the link goes ("see the pricing"), and poor anchor text doesn't ("click here"). It affects both accessibility and SEO.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Anycast</dt><dd>A way of routing traffic in which the same IP address is announced from many places around the world, and each visitor reaches the closest one. My hosting network runs on Anycast, which is why my sites respond in under 100 ms almost everywhere.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>API</dt><dd>Application Programming Interface: a way for two systems to talk to each other. My forms send submissions to a form service's API, and my statistics dashboards read from the hosting network's API. Much of the modern web runs on APIs.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>ARIA</dt><dd>Accessible Rich Internet Applications: a set of HTML attributes that help assistive technology understand unusual interface elements. I use it sparingly, because semantic HTML already does most of the job.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Aspect ratio</dt><dd>The shape of an image or video, width to height (16:9 widescreen, 4:3 classic, or 1:1 square). Setting the aspect ratio in CSS keeps the layout from jumping while images load.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>AVIF</dt><dd>A modern image format that makes photos about 50% smaller than JPEG at the same visual quality. Every site I build serves AVIF first, with WebP and JPEG as fallbacks.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="b">
      <h2>B</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Backlink</dt><dd>A link to your site from someone else's. Google treats backlinks from reputable sites as a sign that yours can be trusted, which can help your ranking.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Backup</dt><dd>A saved copy of your site, so it can be restored if something goes wrong. My sites are stored in Git, so every change is itself a backup, and I can restore any earlier version in seconds.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Bandwidth</dt><dd>The total amount of data your site sends to visitors over time. My hosting doesn't meter bandwidth, so a busy month never brings a surprise bill.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Bot</dt><dd>An automated program that visits websites. Some are useful, like Google's crawler indexing your site, and most are not, like scrapers, spammers, and vulnerability scanners. My hosting network blocks the bad ones before they reach your site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Bounce rate</dt><dd>The share of visitors who land on a page and leave without doing anything else. A high bounce rate isn't always bad (someone who reads an FAQ and gets the answer is fine), but it can point to pages that aren't doing their job.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Breadcrumb</dt><dd>The small trail of links near the top of a page that shows where you are (Home / Services / Roofing). It helps visitors and search engines understand how the site is organized.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Brotli</dt><dd>A modern compression method that shrinks HTML, CSS, and JavaScript on their way to the visitor, 15 to 25% more than gzip. My hosting serves every site with Brotli turned on.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Browser</dt><dd>The program people use to view your site, such as Chrome, Safari, Firefox, or Edge. I test every site on the latest two versions of all four, on desktop and mobile.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Bunny Fonts</dt><dd>A font service that mirrors Google Fonts while respecting privacy: the same fonts, with no cookies, no tracking, and no consent banner. Every site I build uses it by default.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="c">
      <h2>C</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Cache busting</dt><dd>Making browsers download a fresh copy of a file after I change it. I add a content fingerprint to the file's address (for example, <code>style.css?v=4a701c</code>), so each update reaches returning visitors automatically.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Caching</dt><dd>Saving a copy of something so it doesn't have to be fetched or built again. The browser caches images, the CDN caches HTML, and the build caches generated files. Each layer makes the site faster.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Call to action (CTA)</dt><dd>The button or link that asks the visitor to take the next step, such as booking a call, requesting a quote, or downloading a guide. Every page on every site I build has at least one clear, prominent CTA.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Canonical URL</dt><dd>A tag in the page's code that says "this is the official address of this page." It tells search engines to ignore duplicate addresses, such as ones with tracking codes added. Every page on my sites has one.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>CDN</dt><dd>Content Delivery Network: servers around the world that send your site's files from whichever location is closest to the visitor. It's one reason my sites stay in the green on Google's speed test.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Citations (local SEO)</dt><dd>Listings of your business name, address, and phone number on directories such as Yelp, Bing Places, the BBB, and trade-association sites. When they all match, Google can confirm your business is real.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>CLS (Cumulative Layout Shift)</dt><dd>One of Google's Core Web Vitals. It measures how much a page jumps around as it loads. A good score is under 0.1, and I aim for under 0.05.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Cloudflare Pages</dt><dd>The hosting service where every site I build lives by default. It runs on a global network, with unlimited bandwidth at no cost, automatic HTTPS, and one-click rollbacks.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>CMS</dt><dd>Content Management System: software that lets non-technical people edit a website in a browser. WordPress is the most common. I don't use one by default; instead, you email me what you'd like changed.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Color contrast</dt><dd>The difference in brightness between text and its background. WCAG 2.2 AA requires 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. I check every color pair on every site I build.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Compression</dt><dd>Shrinking files so they download faster. Images are compressed visually (AVIF and WebP), and HTML, CSS, and JavaScript are compressed without losing anything (Brotli and gzip). Both happen automatically on every site I build.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Conversion</dt><dd>A visitor doing what you hoped they'd do: filling in a form, calling, or requesting a quote. It's the point of the whole site, expressed as a number you can measure.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Cookie</dt><dd>A small piece of data a browser stores for a site. Some are essential (a theme choice or a login), but most are for tracking. My sites use no tracking cookies by default, so they need no cookie banner.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Copy</dt><dd>The words on the page. Good copy is short, clear, written in your customer's language, and laid out so a skimmer still gets the point. Words do more of a site's work than the design does.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Core Web Vitals</dt><dd>Google's three main speed and stability measures: LCP (loading), INP (response to taps and clicks), and CLS (visual stability). They affect search ranking, and every site I build meets the "good" threshold on all three.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Crawl</dt><dd>What search engines do to find and reread your pages. I make it easy for them with clean addresses, a complete sitemap, no JavaScript barriers, and fast responses.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>CSP (Content Security Policy)</dt><dd>A security header that tells the browser exactly which sources of scripts, styles, images, and frames may load. It stops most cross-site scripting attacks, and I set it tightly on every site I build.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>CSS</dt><dd>Cascading Style Sheets: the language that controls how a site looks (colors, layout, type, and spacing). I write plain CSS with custom properties and no framework.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Custom-coded</dt><dd>A website written from scratch, with no WordPress, no page builder, and no store-bought template. It's faster, more secure, and made for the business it serves.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="d">
      <h2>D</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Dark mode</dt><dd>A darker color scheme for low light. My sites offer light, dark, and "follow system" modes through a toggle in the header, and they remember the choice on your device for the next visit.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>DDoS protection</dt><dd>Defense against distributed denial-of-service attacks, in which thousands of bots try to flood a site offline. My hosting network absorbs these attacks before they reach your site, and the protection comes with every site I host.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Defer</dt><dd>An attribute on script tags that tells the browser to load the script in the background and run it after the page is read. It keeps JavaScript from holding up your content.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Deploy</dt><dd>Putting a new version of the site live. For my sites, it happens automatically when I push a change to the site's code repository, and the hosting builds and publishes it in under 60 seconds.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>DKIM, SPF, DMARC</dt><dd>Three DNS records that stop spammers from sending email that pretends to come from your domain. I set all three at launch for every client domain that sends email.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>DNS</dt><dd>Domain Name System: the service that turns your domain (such as "yourbusiness.com") into the address of the server that holds your site. At launch, we point your DNS at my hosting.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Domain</dt><dd>Your website address (such as "yourbusiness.com"). It's registered in your name, and you control it. I never own your domain; while you're a client, I point its DNS at my hosting.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Domain authority</dt><dd>A score from an outside company (such as Moz or Ahrefs) that estimates how strong your domain is in search. It's useful for tracking a trend, but it isn't an official Google measure, so don't worry much about the number.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="e">
      <h2>E</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>E-E-A-T</dt><dd>Experience, Expertise, Authoritativeness, and Trustworthiness: Google's way of judging content quality, especially on "your money or your life" (YMYL) topics. Your own photos, names, reviews, and credentials beat anonymous content every time.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Edge</dt><dd>The servers closest to your visitors. The "edge" of a CDN is where your site is served from on each visit. My hosting network has more than 300 edge locations worldwide.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Eleventy (11ty)</dt><dd>The static site generator I use to build every site. It's open source, fast, and written in JavaScript, and it turns templates, content, and data into finished HTML pages at build time.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Email deliverability</dt><dd>Whether email from your domain reaches the recipient's inbox instead of the spam folder. It depends on DKIM, SPF, DMARC, and your sending reputation. I set up the records, but I don't manage the email you send.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Email forwarding</dt><dd>A service that sends mail addressed to <code>you@yourdomain.com</code> on to the inbox you already use, such as Gmail. Most registrars include it free, and it's what I recommend if you don't need a full mailbox.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Encryption</dt><dd>Scrambling data so it can't be read without a key. HTTPS encrypts the connection between visitor and site, and password storage encrypts data at rest. I use modern, audited encryption wherever it's needed.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="f">
      <h2>F</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Fallback font</dt><dd>The font shown if your main web font fails to load. I pair every web font with a system font of similar size, so the page doesn't shift when the web font arrives.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Favicon</dt><dd>The small icon in browser tabs, bookmarks, and home-screen shortcuts. I generate a full set (for every device, browser, and variant) automatically at build time.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>FCP (First Contentful Paint)</dt><dd>The time from a visitor clicking your link to seeing anything (text or an image) on the screen. I aim for under 800 ms.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>font-display: swap</dt><dd>A CSS rule that tells the browser to show text in a system font right away, then switch to the web font once it loads. It prevents blank text while fonts load.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Footer</dt><dd>The strip at the bottom of every page. It usually holds the site name, contact details, copyright, and secondary links (privacy, terms, accessibility, and sitemap). My footers are the same on every page, so things are easy to find.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Form spam</dt><dd>Fake submissions sent by bots. Without protection, contact forms get flooded. I block spam with hidden honeypot fields, time-on-page checks, and the form service's built-in filtering.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="g">
      <h2>G</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>GA4</dt><dd>Google Analytics 4: the current version of Google's analytics. It uses cookies, needs consent in the EU, and changed everything from how it counts sessions to how reports are built. It's optional on my sites, and every site comes with two cookie-free statistics tools by default.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>GDPR</dt><dd>General Data Protection Regulation: the EU's privacy law. It requires consent for tracking that uses cookies or shares data outside the EU. The statistics tools I use by default are GDPR-compliant by design.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Git</dt><dd>The version-control system that records every change to your site's code. Each change has a timestamp, an author, and a description, and it can be reversed. Git is the basis for both backups and a full record of changes.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>GitHub</dt><dd>Where I store the code for every site, with each client site in its own private repository. Every change is saved, signed, and reversible.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Google Business Profile (GBP)</dt><dd>Your free business listing on Google Maps and Search. It's separate from your website but closely tied to it. I match your site's structured data to your profile, so Google treats them as the same business.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Google Search Console</dt><dd>Google's free tool that shows how Google sees your site, which searches you appear in, and what errors it finds. I connect it at launch for every client.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Google Tag Manager (GTM)</dt><dd>A tool that lets non-developers add tracking scripts to a site without changing its code. It's powerful, but it adds weight and depends on an outside service. I don't install it by default, and I add only the tags you need.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>gzip / Brotli</dt><dd>Compression methods that shrink files on their way to the visitor. My hosting uses Brotli (slightly better) when the browser supports it, and gzip otherwise.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="h">
      <h2>H</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>H1, H2, H3 hierarchy</dt><dd>The order of headings on a page: one H1 (the page title), H2s for major sections, and H3s for subsections. It matters for both SEO and screen readers, and I keep it correct on every page.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Hash</dt><dd>A short fingerprint made from a file's contents. I use it for cache busting (the <code>?v=4a701c</code> in CSS addresses) and for Subresource Integrity, which proves an outside script hasn't been tampered with.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Headless CMS</dt><dd>A content management system with no built-in front end. It supplies content through an API, and a separate site displays it. I use one only for clients who publish a large volume of content.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Honeypot</dt><dd>A hidden form field that people never see (or fill in) but bots usually do. It catches spam without making people solve a CAPTCHA.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Hosting</dt><dd>The service that stores your website's files and sends them to visitors. Hosting is included in the monthly plan, so you don't deal with a control panel or a separate hosting bill.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>HSTS</dt><dd>HTTP Strict Transport Security: a header that tells browsers to always use HTTPS for your domain. Once a browser has seen it, it refuses plain HTTP connections, even if a malicious link tries one.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>HTTP/2</dt><dd>The previous version of the web's main protocol. It sends many files over one connection, compresses headers, and supports server push, so it's faster than HTTP/1.1, and it's still widely used. My hosting serves HTTP/2 to any browser that doesn't support HTTP/3.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>HTTP/3</dt><dd>The latest version of the protocol that runs the web. It's faster on patchy connections, because losing one packet doesn't stall everything else. My hosting serves every site over HTTP/3 by default.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>HTTPS / SSL</dt><dd>The padlock in the browser bar. It means the connection between the visitor and your site is encrypted. Every site I build has HTTPS from day one.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="i">
      <h2>I</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Iframe</dt><dd>An HTML element that places another web page inside yours, common for maps, video players, and embedded forms. I restrict iframes with a Content Security Policy, so a compromised outside service can't reach into your site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Image optimization</dt><dd>Making images smaller without making them look worse. My build process creates AVIF, WebP, and JPEG versions at several widths, so the browser picks the smallest one that fits the screen.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Indexed</dt><dd>A page is "indexed" once Google has read it and added it to its search results. New pages usually take a few days to a few weeks.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Inline CSS</dt><dd>CSS written inside the HTML instead of loaded from a separate file. I place a small block of "critical" CSS in the page head, so the first paint doesn't wait for a network round trip.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>INP (Interaction to Next Paint)</dt><dd>One of Google's Core Web Vitals. It measures how quickly a page responds when you tap or click. Good is under 200 ms, and I aim for under 100 ms.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Internal link</dt><dd>A link from one page of your site to another. It helps visitors find content and helps search engines see which pages matter most.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>IP address</dt><dd>The numeric address of a computer on the internet (for example, <code>104.21.7.1</code>). DNS turns your domain into one. My hosting network hides your site's origin IP address, which blocks a whole class of direct attacks.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="j">
      <h2>J</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>JAMstack</dt><dd>A way of building sites: pages built ahead of time from JavaScript and markup, served from a CDN, with APIs handling anything dynamic. Every site I build follows it.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>JavaScript</dt><dd>The language that adds interaction to web pages. My sites use plain JavaScript (no framework), under 10 KB on a typical page. The site works without it, and JavaScript makes a few interactions nicer.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>JPEG</dt><dd>The 30-year-old standard image format, supported everywhere. I still deliver it as the last fallback (after AVIF and WebP), so every browser can show the photo.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>JSON-LD</dt><dd>The format I use for structured data: a small block of code in the page head that tells search engines what kind of business you are, where you work, what you charge, and so on. See "Schema markup."</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="k">
      <h2>K</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Keyword</dt><dd>The phrase a customer types into Google ("emergency plumber Denver" or "commercial roof inspection Phoenix"). Good SEO answers the question behind the keyword.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>KPI</dt><dd>Key Performance Indicator: one of the few numbers that matter for your business, such as booked calls, quote requests, and search visits from your service area. Everything else is noise.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="l">
      <h2>L</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Landing page</dt><dd>A single page built for one goal, often paired with an ad campaign. It drops the menu and focuses on one call to action. I can build landing pages on any site I host.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Lazy loading</dt><dd>Waiting to load images and other files until the visitor scrolls near them. Modern browsers support it with <code>loading="lazy"</code>, and it saves a lot of data on long pages.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>LCP (Largest Contentful Paint)</dt><dd>One of Google's Core Web Vitals. It measures how long the largest visible element (usually a hero image or headline) takes to appear. Good is under 2.5 s, and I aim for under 1.0 s.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Lighthouse</dt><dd>Google's open-source checking tool. It runs in Chrome's developer tools or as part of an automated build, and it checks speed, accessibility, SEO, and good practice. I aim for 95&ndash;100 on every measure.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Local SEO</dt><dd>The work of ranking for "near me" and city searches. It depends on your Google Business Profile, directory listings, reviews, and signals on your own site (your address, structured data, and service-area pages). It's where most of my clients' gains come from.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Long-tail keyword</dt><dd>A more specific, less-searched phrase ("flat-roof leak repair contractor in Aurora CO" instead of "roofer"). It has less competition and more buying intent, and it's easier to rank for, which makes it the core of small-business SEO.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="m">
      <h2>M</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Map embed</dt><dd>An interactive map placed on a page. I use an open-source map library with OpenStreetMap maps instead of Google Maps embeds, because it needs no cookies or consent banner.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Markdown</dt><dd>A plain-text writing format that converts to HTML. I use it for blog posts and long articles. It's easy to read as plain text and easy to write without knowing HTML.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Media query</dt><dd>A CSS rule that applies styles only at certain screen sizes (for example, only on screens narrower than 768px). It's how responsive design works.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Meta description</dt><dd>The short summary Google shows under your page title in search results, 50 to 160 characters long and written for the person searching. Every page on every site I build has its own.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Minification</dt><dd>Removing spaces and shortening code in HTML, CSS, and JavaScript without changing how it works, which saves data. I minify all three every time I publish to the live site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Mobile-first</dt><dd>Designing for the phone screen first, then scaling up to larger screens. It's how I build every site, because most service-business visitors are on a phone.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>MX records</dt><dd>The DNS records that say where email for your domain should go. I set them at launch for whichever email provider you choose.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="n">
      <h2>N</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Name servers</dt><dd>The DNS servers in charge of your domain, set at your registrar. When we point your domain at my hosting, I change your DNS records, not your name servers.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>noindex</dt><dd>A tag (or HTTP header) that tells search engines to keep a page out of search results. It's used on internal pages, thank-you pages, and anything unfinished or private.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Nunjucks</dt><dd>The template language I write pages in. It looks like HTML with extra <code>&#123;% %&#125;</code> and <code>&#123;&#123; &#125;&#125;</code> markers, and it lets me reuse layouts, shared pieces, and data across many pages.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="o">
      <h2>O</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>OG image</dt><dd>The preview image that appears when someone shares your link on social media or in a chat app. I create one for every page on every site I build, not only the home page.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>On-page SEO</dt><dd>The parts of SEO that live on your own site: titles, headings, meta descriptions, internal links, structured data, page speed, and accessibility. It's the half of SEO I build directly into your site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Off-page SEO</dt><dd>The parts of SEO that happen elsewhere: backlinks, directory listings, reviews, and Google Business Profile activity. It's the half of SEO you earn over time.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Open Graph</dt><dd>The HTML tags that control how your link looks when someone shares it on Facebook, LinkedIn, Slack, iMessage, or anywhere else. Every page on my sites has them.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Operational pages</dt><dd>The supporting pages every professional site needs: Privacy Policy, Terms of Use, Accessibility Statement, Sitemap, Style Guide, Contact, and Thank-You. Every site I build includes them, and they don't count against your page limit.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Outage</dt><dd>Time when your site can't be reached. My hosting is designed to make outages very rare, and when one happens, my monitoring tells me before you notice.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="p">
      <h2>P</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Page builder</dt><dd>A drag-and-drop tool (such as Elementor or Divi) that sits on top of WordPress and lets non-developers assemble pages. Page builders produce slow, bloated code, which is a major reason I don't use them.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Page weight</dt><dd>The total size of everything a page downloads on first load (HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, and fonts). The industry average is over 2 MB, and my typical page is under 200 KB.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Pageview</dt><dd>One visit to one page, the basic unit of most statistics tools. It's useful for spotting trends, but it can't replace measuring conversions.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Pagefind</dt><dd>The full-text search tool on every site I build. It builds its index when I publish, and searches run entirely in the visitor's browser, with no server, no API, and no cookies.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>PageSpeed</dt><dd>Google's tool for measuring how fast your site loads, scored from 0 to 100 on mobile and desktop. I aim for 95&ndash;100 on every site I build.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Permissions-Policy</dt><dd>A security header that turns off browser features your site doesn't need, such as the camera, microphone, and location. Every site I build denies them by default.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>PNG</dt><dd>An image format that loses no detail, good for logos, icons, and screenshots with crisp edges. It's larger than JPEG for photos and smaller for flat graphics, so it still has its uses.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Preconnect</dt><dd>A hint in the page head that opens a connection to an outside server (such as a font server) before the CSS asks for it. It saves a couple hundred milliseconds for first-time visitors.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>prefers-color-scheme</dt><dd>A CSS media query that detects whether the visitor's device is set to light or dark mode. My sites follow it by default unless the visitor chooses otherwise.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>prefers-reduced-motion</dt><dd>A CSS media query that detects whether the visitor has asked their device to reduce animation, often because motion makes them feel unwell. When it's set, I turn off transitions and animations across the site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Privacy policy</dt><dd>A plain-language description of what data your site collects, how it's used, and who it's shared with. GDPR, CCPA, and most state privacy laws require one. Every site I build has one written for the tools that site uses.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>PurgeCSS</dt><dd>A build-time tool that scans your HTML and removes unused CSS rules from the final stylesheet. It lightens the page without changing how the site looks.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="q">
      <h2>Q</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>QR code</dt><dd>A square barcode that opens a web address when a phone camera scans it. It's useful on vehicle wraps, business cards, and yard signs, and I can make branded QR codes that point to any page on your site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Query string</dt><dd>The part of a web address after the <code>?</code>, as in <code>/landing/?utm_source=google&amp;utm_campaign=spring</code>. It's used for tracking and filtering, and I make sure it never confuses search engines about a page's official address.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="r">
      <h2>R</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Redirect</dt><dd>An automatic forward from one address to another. A 301 is for a permanent move (and keeps your SEO), and a 302 is for a temporary one. I set redirects up on the hosting network, so they're instant and don't depend on a single server.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Registrar</dt><dd>The company you bought your domain from (GoDaddy, Namecheap, Porkbun, Cloudflare Registrar, and others), and where you renew it. Your domain always stays in your name; I never transfer the registration to myself.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Resolution</dt><dd>The pixel size of a screen or image (1920&times;1080, 2x retina, and so on). My build process makes images in the right sizes for every common resolution.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Responsive design</dt><dd>A website that adapts to the screen it's on: phone, tablet, or desktop. I design for phones first, because more than 70% of service-business searches happen on a phone.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>robots.txt</dt><dd>A file at <code>/robots.txt</code> that tells search-engine crawlers what they may and may not index, and where to find the XML sitemap. Every site I build has one.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Rollback</dt><dd>Returning the live site to an earlier version. My hosting keeps every earlier version, and one click puts an old one back. I've never needed it in an emergency, but it's there.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>RSS feed</dt><dd>An XML file at <code>/feed.xml</code> that lists your latest blog posts in a format any reader app can subscribe to. I create one automatically for every site with a blog.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>RUM (Real User Monitoring)</dt><dd>Speed data collected from actual visitors instead of lab tests. The statistics on my hosting network report real-world Core Web Vitals for all your visitors, without cookies.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="s">
      <h2>S</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Schema.org</dt><dd>The shared vocabulary search engines agreed on for structured data, with types such as LocalBusiness, Service, FAQPage, and BreadcrumbList. Every site I build uses the types that fit its content.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Schema markup</dt><dd>Structured data added to your site that helps Google understand what kind of business you are, where you work, and what you charge. I include local-business schema on every site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Screen reader</dt><dd>Software that reads a page aloud for blind and low-vision people (VoiceOver on Apple devices, NVDA and JAWS on Windows, and TalkBack on Android). I test every site with at least one.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Search index</dt><dd>The data (often a JSON file) that on-site search reads from. It's built when I publish, so searches run in the browser without calling a server.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>security.txt</dt><dd>A standard file at <code>/.well-known/security.txt</code> that tells security researchers how to report a vulnerability responsibly. Some industries require it, and every site I build includes it.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>SEO</dt><dd>Search Engine Optimization: building your site so it shows up in Google for the searches your customers make. I build the on-page foundation, and rankings come from that plus time, content, and reviews.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Semantic HTML</dt><dd>Using each HTML element for its intended job: headings for headings, lists for lists, and buttons for buttons. It's the foundation of both accessibility and SEO.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Service area</dt><dd>The region you serve. I build a page for each area with useful local content, instead of thin "doorway" pages, so each one helps visitors and can be indexed.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Sitemap</dt><dd>A file (usually at <code>/sitemap.xml</code>) that lists every page on your site for search engines. I create one automatically and submit it to Google Search Console at launch.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Skip link</dt><dd>The "Skip to main content" link that comes first when you tab through a page. It lets keyboard and screen-reader users jump past the menu, and WCAG 2.2 AA requires it.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Slug</dt><dd>The last part of a web address (the <code>about-our-team</code> in <code>/about-our-team/</code>): lowercase, hyphenated, and descriptive. I choose slugs carefully, because addresses affect SEO.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Source map</dt><dd>A separate file that links minified code back to the original, which helps with debugging. I include source maps on test versions of a site and turn them off on the live site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>SRI (Subresource Integrity)</dt><dd>An attribute that lets the browser check, by its fingerprint (hash), that an outside script hasn't been tampered with. I use it on every outside file whose source supports it.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>SSG (Static Site Generator)</dt><dd>A tool that turns templates and content into finished HTML pages at build time. Eleventy is the one I use. It's the opposite of a CMS that builds each page on every visit.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Structured data</dt><dd>Labels in a page's code that let search engines show rich results (star ratings, prices, FAQs, and opening hours). I write it in the JSON-LD format with the schema.org vocabulary.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Subdomain</dt><dd>A name added in front of your main domain (such as <code>blog.yourbusiness.com</code> or <code>shop.yourbusiness.com</code>). It's useful for side projects under your brand without buying a new domain.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>SVG</dt><dd>Scalable Vector Graphics: an image format made of shapes defined by math instead of pixels. SVGs stay sharp at any size and are tiny for icons and logos, so I use them wherever I can.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>System font</dt><dd>A font already installed on the visitor's device (San Francisco on Apple, Segoe UI on Windows, and Roboto on Android). It loads instantly, and I use it as the fallback until the web font arrives.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="t">
      <h2>T</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>TBT (Total Blocking Time)</dt><dd>How long a page can't respond to input while it first loads. I aim for under 100 ms.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Theme toggle</dt><dd>The light, dark, and system control in my site headers. It saves your choice on your device, so the next visit applies it before the page appears, with no flash of the wrong theme.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Title tag</dt><dd>The HTML <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> element: the line shown in the browser tab and as the headline in search results. It's the most important on-page SEO element, and I write one for every page.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Touch target</dt><dd>The tappable size of a button or link. WCAG 2.2 AA requires at least 24&times;24 CSS pixels, and I make every interactive element 44&times;44, the practical minimum for fingers.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Tracking pixel</dt><dd>A 1&times;1 image (or hidden script) that loads from an outside tracker (Meta, Google Ads, and others) and reports the visit back. I add only the pixels you need, and only after listing them in your privacy policy.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>TTFB (Time to First Byte)</dt><dd>The time from a visitor's click to the first byte of the page arriving. My hosting network keeps it under 100 ms for most visitors in North America.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="u">
      <h2>U</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Umami</dt><dd>Open-source visitor statistics with no cookies. It shows the same data Google Analytics does (page views, referrers, devices, and events) without the cookie banner or the privacy concerns. It's included on every monthly-plan site I build, and every client gets a private live dashboard link on launch day.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Uptime</dt><dd>The share of time your site is reachable. My hosting runs at 99.99% or better, checked every minute from three regions, so I know about a problem before you notice it.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>URL</dt><dd>Uniform Resource Locator: the full web address of a page, including the protocol, domain, path, and any query string. It's how the web identifies a page.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>UX (User Experience)</dt><dd>Everything about how a visitor experiences your site: how fast it loads, whether it's clear what to do next, and whether the form works when they send it. It decides whether they become a customer.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="v">
      <h2>V</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Validator</dt><dd>A tool that checks your HTML, CSS, or structured data for errors. The W3C runs the standard HTML and CSS validators, and Google runs one for structured data. I run all three before launch.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Variable fonts</dt><dd>One font file that holds every weight and width as adjustable settings. It downloads faster than separate files for each weight, so I use variable fonts whenever a family offers them.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Vendor lock-in</dt><dd>When the platform you're on makes it costly or impossible to leave, for example through a proprietary page builder, a closed CMS, or custom plugins. With me, your domain stays registered in your name and under your control. I own the code on the monthly plan, and if you cancel, I help you move your domain to a registrar you choose.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Viewport</dt><dd>The size of the browser window showing your site. Phone viewports are usually 360&ndash;430 px wide, and desktops 1280&ndash;1920 px. My designs adapt smoothly across the whole range.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="w">
      <h2>W</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Waterfall (loading)</dt><dd>The chart in a browser's developer tools that shows every file a page loads, in time order. It reveals what's blocking, what's slow, and what's repeated, so it's the standard way to find speed problems.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>WCAG 2.2 AA</dt><dd>Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, version 2.2, Level AA: the W3C's accessibility standard. It covers contrast, keyboard navigation, screen-reader support, and more, and it's my working baseline on every site.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Web font</dt><dd>A font loaded from the web (for example, from a font service) instead of one installed on the device. It lets you use your brand's typography while the page still loads quickly.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Web3Forms</dt><dd>A lightweight outside service that handles contact-form submissions, so your site needs no server of its own. It's fast and resists spam with a honeypot and rate limits, and every one of my sites uses it for forms.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>WebP</dt><dd>An image format that makes photos about 25% smaller than JPEG. Every browser since 2020 supports it, and I serve it when a browser can't show AVIF.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>WHOIS</dt><dd>The public registry that lists who owns each domain and when it expires. I watch WHOIS expiration dates myself, so renewal reminders don't depend on the registrar's email.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>Wireframe</dt><dd>A rough sketch of a page's layout, with boxes for content and lines for text, used to agree on structure before visual design. Instead of formal wireframes, I give you a working draft you can click through.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>WordPress</dt><dd>A content management system that runs about 40% of the internet and is the most-hacked platform on the web. I don't build on it, for security and speed reasons.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="x">
      <h2>X</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>XML sitemap</dt><dd>The machine-readable list of every page on your site, in XML format, at <code>/sitemap.xml</code>. I submit it to Google Search Console at launch, so new pages are found quickly.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>XSS (Cross-Site Scripting)</dt><dd>An attack that slips harmful code into a page so it runs in your visitors' browsers. Safe templates and a tight Content Security Policy prevent most of it, and I build both into every site by default.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="glossary-section" id="y">
      <h2>Y</h2>
      <dl>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>YMYL</dt><dd>"Your Money or Your Life": Google's name for topics where bad information can hurt people (health, finance, legal matters, and home safety). Google holds these pages to a higher standard, which is worth knowing if your business touches any of them.</dd></div>
        <div class="glossary-entry"><dt>YouTube embed</dt><dd>An iframe that places a YouTube player on your page. It adds a lot of weight and tracking, so I use a click-to-load thumbnail instead, and the player loads only when a visitor presses play.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

